A||||4||Eric||End Links
Z||000000||Eric||06-13-99||04:24 PM||||I was considering buying new polyurathane endlinks for my 88GT if for no other reason, stiffin the suspension and because they are pretty economical.  I was looking at my existing enlinks, expecting to find the black rubber bushing I see everywhere else on the car, when I noticed they weren't the typical black rubber bushing.  They were clear under the layer of crap from the road.  I don't know what kind of material this is but it seems to be a little stiffer than typical rubber.  Are they stock?  Would I still benifit from polyurathane?
Z||000001||mwbackus||06-14-99||01:24 AM||||     The endlink bushings on my formula were not rubber.  They were kind of a hard yellowish tinted animal.  New endlink bushings are cheap, I would replace them with poly if they show signs of wear or distortion.  The endlink itself may not need to be replaced, but if they are original with average miles, I would replace the entire assembly. <br>     I have seen endlinks with poly busings made by addco at many local discount parts shops for around $13 a pair.  You could just get them through Suspension Restoration, but you would probably end up paying as much for shipping costs as you would for the parts themselves. 
Z||000002||lowCG||06-14-99||01:57 AM||||your endlinks are probably already urethane,or something better,how rigid are they?
Z||000003||Eric||06-14-99||07:25 AM||||There are just like mwbackus said.  They are harder than rubber and yellowish clear.  I noticed the front squeaks when it's really cold out.  If they are original, they have 140000 miles on them.
Z||000004||theogre||06-14-99||07:30 AM||||sounds like they are 'raw' polly. i.e. there's no dye in them.<p>do all the sway bushings, not just the end links. make sure everything is very clean, and use an apropriate grease. doing all the bushings can give the same result as adding a stiffer bar and using rubber.
